> Actual conflicts refuse arbitrary parsing

Retain the original left-recursive control E→E+T | T, T→T*F | F, F→(E) | id. Its
real construction attempts put distinct rule IDs into the same lookahead cells.
The earliest rule remains visible, but using it as an arbitrary recognizer can
repeatedly expand E without consuming input. The corrected API therefore permits
conflict inspection while rejecting a parse request with a logic error. This
refusal is not a statement that the expression itself lies outside the
context-free language.

The next table prints every actual conflicting cell and both original rule
identities. The diagram uses its first native witness. No fallback rule choice
or successful parse is invented for the conflicted model.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ll1_conflict.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/ll1_parser.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const dm::algorithms::LL1Parser p(dm::algorithms::ll1_expression_grammar(
                                      true));
    std::print("| actual conflict nonterminal | actual lookahead | actual earliest retained rule | actual " "conflicting incoming rule |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &c : p.conflicts())
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", c.nonterminal, c.lookahead,
                   c.retained, c.incoming);
    bool refused = false;
    try {
        p.recognize({"id"});
    } catch (const std::logic_error &) {
        refused = true;
    }
    std::print("\nActual grammar conflict-free {}; actual parse request refused {}.\n",
               p.is_ll1(), refused);
    auto c = p.conflicts().front();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("retained", {0, 2},
           std::format("Actual retained production\nrule{}", c.retained)).node(
        "incoming", {0, -2},
        std::format("Actual incoming production\nrule{}", c.incoming)).node(
        "cell", {7, 0},
        std::format("Actual conflicting cell\n({}, {})", c.nonterminal,
                    c.lookahead)).edge("retained", "cell").edge("incoming",
                                                                "cell");
    d.save_svg("build/ll1_conflict.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual conflict nonterminal | actual lookahead | actual earliest retained rule | actual conflicting incoming rule |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
| E | ( | 0 | 1 |
| E | id | 0 | 1 |
| T | ( | 2 | 3 |
| T | id | 2 | 3 |

Actual grammar conflict-free false; actual parse request refused true.
\image(figures/ll1_conflict.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual original left-recursive table conflicts and explicit parse refusal.)
\alttext(Two real indexed productions compete for one native cell; refusal avoids silently selecting a potentially nonconsuming left-recursive expansion.)

> Incomplete budgets and complete-input guards

An explicit action budget prevents an unbounded parser loop from being mistaken
for a completed computation. StepLimit is a separate status; a false accepted
flag in that status is not a language rejection. The following independent
budget controls all use the same a,b input under S→a*S*b | epsilon. They are
separate bounded executions, not prefix reruns used to fabricate the earlier
trace. The default recognize method throws if its100000-action budget is
exhausted rather than silently returning false.

Literal empty and dollar tokens are rejected before parsing. This closes the old
early-end-marker acceptance hazard, where a caller-supplied dollar could hide a
remaining tail. Unknown ordinary tokens instead lead to actual no-entry or
mismatch syntax rejection. There is no lexer, error recovery or backtracking.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ll1_budget.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/ll1_parser.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const dm::algorithms::LL1Parser p({{{"S", {"a", "S", "b"}}, {"S", {}}}, "S"});
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| explicit action budget | actual performed actions | actual accepted | actual " "status0:accept,1:no-entry,2:mismatch,3:extra,4:budget | actual consumed tokens |\n| ---: | " "---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t budget : {0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 20}) {
        auto a = p.analyze({"a", "b"}, budget);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", budget, a.steps, a.accepted,
                   int(a.status), a.consumed);
        x.push_back(budget);
        y.push_back(a.steps);
    }
    bool reserved = false;
    try {
        p.recognize({"a", "$", "b"});
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        reserved = true;
    }
    auto unknown = p.analyze({"z"});
    std::print("\nActual reserved end-marker token rejected {}; actual ordinary unknown-token status {}; " "accepted {}.\n",
               reserved, int(unknown.status), unknown.accepted);
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.points(x, y, "actual actions", {37, 99, 235}, 6).y_limits(-.3, 7).title("Actual bounded runs distinguish incompleteness from rejection").x_label("explicit independent action budget").y_label("actual actions performed").save_svg("build/ll1_budget.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| explicit action budget | actual performed actions | actual accepted | actual status0:accept,1:no-entry,2:mismatch,3:extra,4:budget | actual consumed tokens |
| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | false | 4 | 0 |
| 1 | 1 | false | 4 | 0 |
| 2 | 2 | false | 4 | 1 |
| 4 | 4 | false | 4 | 2 |
| 6 | 5 | true | 0 | 2 |
| 20 | 5 | true | 0 | 2 |

Actual reserved end-marker token rejected true; actual ordinary unknown-token status 1; accepted false.
\image(figures/ll1_budget.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual independent action-budget outcomes and reserved-token guard.)
\alttext(Acceptance requires the real final end-marker action; explicit StepLimit outcomes are separate from syntax rejection, and injected end markers cannot hide trailing input.)

> Contracts and verification

Grammar start must be a defined nonterminal, every literal lhs/rhs symbol is
nonempty and differs from the reserved dollar marker, and rule IDs fit int.
Empty rhs represents epsilon. FIRST/FOLLOW terminate through finite monotone
insertions; table construction records actual conflicts and retains earliest
indexed rules for inspection. Parsing refuses conflicted models. The owned
grammar, final sets, table and witnesses are available as const model-lifetime
references.

Parsing performs actual stack actions and table/member lookups, with
grammar-dependent preprocessing and lookup/push costs. For fixed suitable
grammar, productive parsing has the usual linear token-length interpretation,
but no universal O(tokens) claim covers arbitrary grammar sizes, nullable
expansion counts, map/string comparisons or explicit budget exhaustion.
Construction and action counters name their selected real operations rather than
elapsed time.

Trace counts original token symbols, two actual initial stack symbols, each real
event record and its actual before/after stack symbols. Initial and per-event
known geometry is admitted before copies. Default cap is one million logical
records;0 disables only the optional capture cap. String characters, scalar
fields/capacities/runtime/result copies and bytes/RAM are excluded.
Action-budget status remains explicit, captures own their data and no replay
reconstructs decisions.

Native tests compare 2047 short binary words with the independently defined a^n
b^n language, verify real parser actions and FIRST/FOLLOW sets, and cover
conflicting tables, reserved tokens, explicit budgets, quotas and ownership. The
ten-report batch passed 101 native CTest cases, 34 directly instrumented
ASan/UBSan cases, 103 compiled/executed public examples, 515/515 scoped file
definitions plus 10 exact LCG class definitions, and ten representative clangd
hovers. Independent Python integer/Fraction, explicit-label graph search,
quadratic sequence DP and full weighted-stump references passed 69430 records
across all ten topics, including a separately fitted held-out Iris model. Frozen
ordinary compatibility passed 1000 Lex-BFS orders, 1000 library sorts, 100 LBG
codebooks, 100 complete five-round LogitBoost ensembles and 200 LLL bases;
corrected LBG final labels/objectives are intentionally excluded from historical
compatibility. All 4 complete programs executed and all 4 native figures were
rendered and visually reviewed, with clipped legends corrected and rerun.
HTML/Markdown computed-result and figure-byte checks gate legacy retirement.
Repeatable receipts are retained in `build/mepml-conversion/batch-204-213/`.

> References

P. M. Lewis II and R. E. Stearns(1968),
[Syntax-Directed Transduction|https://doi.org/10.1145/321466.321477], Journal of
the ACM15(3),465–488. This report implements explicit FIRST/FOLLOW indexed-table
prediction with finite-budget and conflict contracts, not a general context-free
recognizer or grammar transformation algorithm.
